ng  these  uh translocation events even as we move  all the way down into 3x  coverage which is really really really  low coverage  and then for the the false negative  which is a known translocation event  between  uh chromosome five and two we can see  that as we  kind of down sample we lose the  uh we maintain the contacts between 46  x and 6x coverage and then that signal  kind of goes away  at 3x so we're we're  fairly positive here that we are  we maintain a high degree of sensitivity  even as we move all the way down into 3x  coverage now for the  those translocation events that uh are  false positives that haven't yet been  characterized but we actually  have contact matrix support for them is  uh this one particular one in hcc 1187  which is between chromosome 4 and  chromosome 8  so on these contact matrixes the one on  the left  is from the control line which is the  htc 1187bl  and we're plotting the contacts between  chromosome 4 and chromosome 8  on the y-axis and then the one next to  that is the  hcc 1187 cancerous line  and as you can see in the control line  there is really no  bright spot here in the contact matrix  it all kind of looks like background  noise  but when we look at the hcc 1187 line on  the  right we see this really bright spot  that pops out  uh at us that basically indicates that  the very sort of start of chromosome 4  is interacting with a region of  chromosome 8 between  about 15 megabases to about 45 megabases  uh along the uh along chromosome 8.  so we we do have very kind of strong  evidence here that this  is a true structural variation that has  yet to be characterized in the cell line  and our next steps here are to go in and  pcr this out and just confirm that it  is in fact a a true signal  so uh i'm bringing this sort of coverage  image back up  here because we're about to dial in into  the  the snip abilities of this data type as  well  so previous versions of this high cn  product  single nucleotide variants and that has  again there are gaps in coverage and our  omni-c as you can see again  at the bottom we're recapitulating  shotgun like coverage  meaning that we should be able to call  snips  and single nucleotide variations at a  rate that is similar to that of shotgun  data  and so we've done that here with the  cell line gm12878 so we're looking at  the table on the left which are snips  that were identified in the cell line  gm12878 using gatk  and we have true positive false negative  and then sensitivity and precision for  each for the columns and then the  rows are shotgun data the obviously data  and then restriction enzyme based hi-c  data  and basically the key thing that we want  to point out here is that the  sensitivity and precision  of obviously in being able to detect  snips  is very close to that of shotgun data  which is what we expect based on the  coverage  and it far outstrips the  restriction enzyme-based hi-c data  now the table on the right is asking can  we  successfully phase the haplotypes in  gm12878  uh with the snips that have been  identified through  the omnic data set and comparing that  again to shotgun data and high c data  and so basically the punch line here is  that omni-c data that our  sort of sequence independent uh approach  to fragmenting the genome  is able to capture more heterozygous  sniffs  than single or research and enzyme-based  high-seed which is what we expect based  on the coverage  and we have a much lower switch error  rate than what we see with  again other high c approaches it's not  as good as shotgun but it is  much better than other high c approaches  but one of the things that we like to  point out here  is that you can phase chromosomes end to  end  with the proximity ligation data which  is something that you can't do with  shotgun data because your insert size  with shotgun data is about 1kb  at best with the proximity ligation  approach  your insert size is basically the range  of the chromosome  and so if you