ng it to a  reference genome you can build your own  that's informative around those  structural variation events  and just sort of as a proof of principle  here  we've looked at how this phasing works  between gm12878 and the hg002 genome  and basically what you're looking at  here are k-mers that are coming from  the mom on the y-axis versus k-mers that  are coming from the  paternal line on the x-axis and you can  basically see that  we're we're very much able to separate  out these k-mers  into their specific haplotypes so again  kind of recapitulating the  the idea that we can successfully build  uh  uh deployed assembly from scratch  so leveraging that uh the snip ability  along with that long range information  where  we're really able to build these diploid  assemblies  and one of the last things that we  really want to point out here  is that you can hit this date  this data type with a hybrid capture  panel historically because uh  hi-c data use restriction enzymes you  had to  really redesign your probe set to  incorporate a restriction site  because we don't rely on those you can  take off the  the shelf products here like extra  lockdown  and truesight or really any other um  hybrid capture panel and apply it to the  data and so on the left here what i'm  showing is a workflow of how you'd go  from your sample to your  to your hybrid capture approach and then  how you would process that data  and on the right we're showing the  different types of  uh products that we know we're  compatible with  and then on the bottom we're showing a  one of those contact matrixes that are  reflective of topology  and then we're on the bottom we're  showing  data that has been enriched for rpl5  using the extra lockdown series  and i've been able to not only enrich  the data for that one specific gene with  the rpl5 but we're now  able to ask the question what other  genomic regions  does rpl5 have significant interactions  with  which are depicted there by that arc so 